{
    "title": "Air Plan Approval; Connecticut; Plan Submittals for the 2008 Ozone National Ambient Air Quality Standard",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 11, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-11/pdf/2025-22595.pdf",
    "summary": "The Environmental Protection Agency published two air quality actions: a housekeeping fix to Connecticut\u2019s ozone plan and approval of new sulfur dioxide limits for a lime plant in Ohio.\n\nFor Connecticut, the agency corrected a past typo in federal rules and restored a missing line that confirms it had already approved the state\u2019s motor vehicle inspection and maintenance program for areas that struggle with ozone pollution. This does not change any earlier decisions, add new requirements, or affect drivers\u2019 current inspection routines. It\u2019s simply a cleanup to make the official record accurate and consistent.\n\nWho benefits from the Connecticut action? State and local air agencies, cities, and businesses get clearer, more reliable rules, which helps with planning and enforcement. Residents benefit indirectly because clarity reduces the risk of delays or disputes in carrying out air quality work. There are no practical downsides here; it\u2019s an administrative correction with no new costs.\n\nIn Ohio, the agency approved state-set limits on sulfur dioxide from two kilns at the Carmeuse Lime Maple Grove facility in Seneca County. Bringing these limits into the federal plan makes them enforceable under both state and federal law. The goal is to help keep local air below the health standard for sulfur dioxide, a pollutant that can irritate the lungs, especially for children, older adults, and people with asthma.\n\nWho benefits from the Ohio action? Nearby communities may see cleaner air and fewer health risks. State and local planners gain regulatory certainty, which helps them show the area is meeting the national standard. The company may face added costs to meet the limits or adjust operations, and that could ripple to suppliers or customers. Supporters will point to public health gains and predictability; critics may raise concerns about compliance expenses and potential impacts on production.\n\nOverall, one action tidies up the rulebook in Connecticut, and the other tightens a specific source of pollution in Ohio\u2014both aimed at keeping air quality protections clear and on track.",
